From: Andrew Davis <1709934+Savid@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:44:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ai): keep caching when a client leaves out older thinking

Anthropic lets a request leave out the oldest thinking blocks, as long as it
keeps them out, and a client that keeps only the newest assistant turn's
thinking does exactly that on every request. Each block it leaves out
changes every prefix after it. The rolling breakpoints sit on the tail and
on the newest assistant turn, both after the thinking the next request
leaves out, so no entry they write is ever read again: each request writes
its whole history past the head to the cache.

Through the auth gateway, a 30-step Claude Haiku 5.5 tool loop that kept
only the newest thinking read 14.4k tokens a step from the cache and wrote
15.5k, against 31.7k read and 475 written when it kept every block. From
step 14 on, 13 of 17 requests read nothing past the head.

When a request holds thinking in exactly one assistant turn, with an earlier
assistant turn holding none, the second message breakpoint now goes on the
message before that turn: the last one whose prefix the next request
repeats. It takes priority over decimation, the per-call mark and the second
trailing message. Requests without thinking, or with thinking in more than
one turn, are anchored as before. A conversation that keeps every block takes
the new shape only while its model has thought once, and the anchor then
falls on a message an earlier request already anchored as its tail.

